# Project Overview

This project involved designing and building an autonomous cube-collecting robot for MAE 3780 Mechatronics. The robot was designed to navigate a game board using QTI edge sensors and a color sensor while collecting cubes using a front intake mechanism.

Our original design included:
- A one-way flap collection system
- Ultrasonic cube detection
- Sensor-based autonomous navigation
- A lightweight cardboard chassis with extended intake arms

As development progressed, several mechanical and electrical constraints forced iterative redesigns. The final robot used a simplified forward-only collection strategy with extended intake arms and color-based recovery behavior.

Despite sensor inconsistencies during competition, the robot remained mechanically reliable and successfully demonstrated autonomous navigation and embedded control integration.

# Key Features

- Autonomous navigation using QTI and color sensors
- PWM motor control with Arduino timer registers
- Embedded C programming with interrupt-based sensor handling
- Adaptive recovery logic for board color transitions
- Custom cardboard intake and chassis design

# Challenges & Iteration

One of the largest challenges was balancing sensor reliability with mechanical constraints. Environmental lighting conditions caused unexpected QTI behavior during competition, requiring on-the-fly modifications to the robot’s navigation strategy.

The project also involved extensive iterative redesign:
- Replacing a failed hinged intake system
- Removing the ultrasonic sensor due to power limitations
- Redesigning wheel integration after dimensional conflicts
- Adjusting sensor positioning to prevent drag and wheel interference

These constraints ultimately pushed the project toward a more robust and simplified autonomous strategy.

